# Global Systems First (ABSOLUTE — ALWAYS ON)

**Highest-priority engineering rule for this repo.** Applies to **every** change type — **new code, bug fixes, refactors, cleanups, perf work, data edits, and tests that touch production** — not only skills, and not only when the user repeats it.

Refactor, cleanup, or "small fix" is not a waiver. Same mandate as a new feature: **one owner, one path, delete the wrong path in the same change.**

The project owner must **not** have to re-explain this. Agents enforce it on **every turn** without being asked.

---

## Priority Order (When Rules Conflict)

1. **This rule** + `.cursor/rules/move-preview-intent-truth.mdc` + `.cursor/rules/subagent-council-loop.mdc` + `.cursor/rules/qa-after-gameplay-changes.mdc`
2. Roadmap / phase gates / architecture skills (`Simulator`, layers, timeline, `AbilitySystem`)
3. Convenience, speed, "small local fix," model-default shortcuts

If a proposed edit violates (1) to satisfy (3) → **do not implement** until redesigned or the owner approves a documented exception.

---

## Core Mandate: Every Edit Must

1. **Use the existing global system** that owns the behavior (sim, director, `AbilitySystem`, commit slots, data definitions, overlay derived from slots — not parallel logic).
2. **Minimize heuristics** — no second state machines, no UI-only flags that commit path ignores, no cursor/preview/click paths that disagree.
3. **Extend the canonical path** — one truth pipeline; preview == execution where applicable.
4. **Prefer data** over `if ability.id == …` / per-unit branches.
5. **Remove the obsolete path** in the same change when replacing behavior.
6. **Fail loud** — assert or reject; never silently swallow invalid state.

---

## Definition: Bandaids & Heuristics (Forbidden)

A **heuristic or bandaid** means behavior driven by a local shortcut that is **not** the same rule the rest of the feature uses. A change is a bandaid if it:
- Papers over a bug with an extra `if`, flag, or early `return` without fixing **why** the main path failed.
- Duplicates logic that already exists (or should exist) in a manager, `AbilitySystem`, `commit_from_slots`, or sim preview.
- Adds per-skill / per-unit / per-scene branches when data, targeting, or the shared pipeline can express it.
- Introduces a second source of truth (e.g. cursor heuristics that disagree with commit slots).
- Disables validation, asserts, or tests "for now".
- Leaves dead or overlapping paths (old + new) instead of deleting the wrong one in the same change.
- Wires new features through presentation-only state instead of the domain owner (`AbilitySystem`, commit slots, `Simulator`, data).

### Examples That Are Heuristics (Forbidden)

| Pattern | Example (this repo) |
|---|---|
| Tab- or screen-only apply | UI scale preview only on Interface tab; Display tab unchanged |
| Node/widget identity at apply time | `if lbl == _ui_scale_label` instead of shared applier / data tier |
| Parallel apply paths | `save_to_disk` + `changed` but combat never reloads; separate preview function |
| Presentation-only state | Slider moves a local var UI reads; `GameSettings` / commit path unchanged |
| Per-id / per-scene branch | `if ability.id == …` when `.tres` / `EffectData` could express it |

### Examples That Are Not Heuristics (Canonical Owners)

| Pattern | Example (this repo) |
|---|---|
| Single shared applier | `GameSettings` → `MenuInterfaceApplier.apply(root, settings)` on whole tree |
| Data/meta at build, type rules at apply | `menu_font_tier` meta + walk by `Control` type — not `if node == _foo` |
| One reload+apply bus | `EventBus.interface_settings_changed` → owner `_on_display_settings_applied` |

